-**Pitfalls:**
-*   **Non-manifold topology:** Clustering topologically connected vertices without additional constraints may yield non-manifold topology (e.g., threads collapsing to single polygons or non-manifold edges) [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Subtopics:**
-*   [Manifold Dual Contouring](2138-manifold-dual-contouring.md): Adds topology constraints to prevent non-manifold collapses (e.g., threads collapsing to single polygons) which this method may produce. Choose this when manifold output is strictly required.
-*   [Octree-Based Dual Contouring (3D)](1417-octree-based-dual-contouring-3d.md): Uses a different approach to octree-based dual contouring. This method specifically uses vertex clustering to allow arbitrary vertices per cell.
+### Parameters
+*   **error_threshold ($\epsilon$):** The maximum allowed QEF error for a vertex to be considered collapsible. If the error associated with a vertex is less than this threshold, it is marked as collapsible [S1][S2, unofficial].
+
+### Pitfalls and Edge Cases
+*   **Non-Manifold Topology:** Clustering topologically connected vertices without additional constraints may yield non-manifold topology (e.g., threads collapsing to single polygons or non-manifold edges) [S1][S2, unofficial]. This is addressed in the sibling article [Manifold Dual Contouring](2138-manifold-dual-contouring.md).
+*   **Degenerate Polygons:** If the resulting polygon collapses to an edge or a vertex, it must be discarded [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** QEF minimization requires solving a linear system. Numerical stability should be considered, especially for flat regions where the QEF matrix may be ill-conditioned [G].
+*   **Arbitrary Intersections:** The method can handle an arbitrary number of intersections per edge, which is an advantage over some other methods [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces polygon count in flat regions by allowing arbitrary vertices per cell. Optimizations (collapsed cells/faces) prune the traversal [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** May produce non-manifold topology (e.g., threads collapsing to single polygons) if no additional constraints are applied [S1, unofficial]. Requires careful handling of topological connectivity [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When a simplified, multi-resolution mesh is desired and non-manifold artifacts are acceptable or can be filtered [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When strict manifold topology is required without post-processing [S1, unofficial].
+
+*   **Time Complexity:** The clustering phase involves a bottom-up traversal of the octree. For each non-leaf cell, vertices are clustered by combining QEFs and minimizing them. The complexity depends on the number of vertices and the depth of the octree. The polygon generation phase also traverses the octree, but can be optimized by truncating collapsed cells [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the vertex tree, QEFs for each vertex, and octree structure. The vertex tree can grow significantly if many vertices are not collapsed [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Scaling:** Scales with the resolution of the octree and the complexity of the surface. Flat regions are simplified more aggressively, reducing polygon count [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:**
+    *   Reduces polygon count in flat regions [S1][S2, unofficial].
+    *   Allows arbitrary number of vertices per cell, unlike standard Dual Contouring [S1][S2, unofficial].
+    *   Topologically safe clustering (though may produce non-manifold results without additional constraints) [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   May produce non-manifold topology [S1][S2, unofficial].
+    *   Requires Hermite data (normals and intersections) [S1][S2, unofficial].
+    *   Optimization via collapsed cells requires careful implementation to ensure correctness [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When a multi-resolution iso-surface is desired, and the surface has large flat regions that can be simplified. Suitable when Hermite data is available.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When manifold topology is strictly required (use [Manifold Dual Contouring](2138-manifold-dual-contouring.md) instead). When Hermite data is not available.
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of dual contouring simplification, specifically addressing the restriction of one vertex per cell in standard DC [S1, unofficial]. It builds on vertex clustering techniques similar to Zhang et al. [7] but uses a vertex tree for topological connectivity [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to allow arbitrary numbers of vertices per cell, enabling more aggressive simplification [S1, unofficial].
+
+*   **Origin:** The method was introduced in the paper "Manifold Dual Contouring" by Yu et al. [S1][S2, unofficial]. It builds upon the Dual Contouring (DC) method, which was restrictive in allowing only one vertex per cell [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **History:** The method was developed to address the large number of polygons in flat regions produced by uniform contouring algorithms [S1][S2, unofficial]. It uses the recursive octree traversal algorithm from Ju et al. [3] to efficiently find topologically connected vertices [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Usage:** Used in surface extraction from volumetric data, particularly when adaptive resolution is beneficial [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
